<pb no="402"/>
<term lang="en">
<heading>
Some peculiar Words used by the Vulgar
in SURREY.
</heading>
</term>

<section type="Appendix">
<wordgroup1 type="a">
<wordentry type="h"><form lang="en">Yarrow, yare,</form> <xpln lang="en"> (i.e.) shye.</xpln></wordentry>

<wordentry type="h"><form lang="en">Eve,</form> <xpln lang="en"> (i.e.) modest, or meek.</xpln>
<lemenote>
"eve" not found in OED in this sense.
</lemenote>
</wordentry>

<wordentry type="h"><form lang="en">Spood,</form> <xpln lang="en"> (i.e.) speed.</xpln></wordentry>

<wordentry type="h"><form lang="en">Nott,</form> <xpln lang="en"> (i.e.) sheere, as sheer cut off.</xpln></wordentry>

<wordentry type="h"><form lang="en">Druxen at Heart,</form> <xpln lang="en"> (i.e.) rotten at Heart.</xpln>
<lemenote>
"druxen" not found in OED but likely related to "druxy," a.
</lemenote>
</wordentry>

<wordentry type="h"><form lang="en">Stover,</form> <xpln lang="en"> (i.e.) in a Fret, or Rage.</xpln>
<lemenote>
"strover" not found in OED in this sense.
</lemenote>
</wordentry>

<wordentry type="h"><form lang="en">To foreslowe the Time,</form> <xpln lang="en"> (i.e.) to be re&#173;
miss, to be backward, and lose an Op&#173;
portunity.</xpln></wordentry>

<wordentry type="h"><form lang="en">Pre,</form> <xpln lang="en"> (i.e.) a Plank laid a-cross a Chan&#173;
nel or Gutter to go over, which, in 
other Countries is called a Bridge.</xpln>
<lemenote>
"pre" is possibly a form of "pier," n.
</lemenote>
</wordentry>

<wordentry type="h"><form lang="en">Hatch,</form> <xpln lang="en"> (i.e.) a Gate in the Roads;
and a half Hatch is where a Horse may
pass, but not a Cart.</xpln></wordentry>

<pb no="403"/>

<wordentry type="h"><form lang="en">Place,</form> <xpln lang="en"> (i.e.) the Manour-House.</xpln></wordentry>

<wordentry type="h"><form lang="en">Gill,</form> <xpln lang="en"> (i.e.) a litter narrow Valley with
Wood, and a little Rill running in the
Bottom.</xpln></wordentry>
</wordgroup1>
</section>

<section type="Dedication">
<term lang="en">
<heading>Mr. AUBREY'S Dedication to his de&#173;
signed Natural History of WILTSHIRE.

To the Right Honourable THOMAS Earl
of PEMBROKE and MONTGOMERY,
Lord HERBERT of CAERDIFFE, one 
of the Privy-Council to their Majesties,
President of the Royal Society, &#38;c. 

My singular good Lord.</heading>
</term>
